WEBVTT
TITLE Deterministic Push-down Automata and DCFLs: One Move per Configuration — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A deterministic push-down automaton is a push-down automaton with an empty choice.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Condition one: one instruction per state, symbol and stack top.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Condition two: if epsilon applies, no symbol may apply to the same cell.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Four tables, three verdicts: 0, 3, 2 and 1 violations.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Five instructions carry the whole machine.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Eighteen cells are examined; five of them hold an instruction.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The epsilon-instruction at (q1, Z) is legal, because nothing else lives there.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
One extra branch costs the title: the language is the same, the class is not.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
aaabbb takes seven steps: three pushes, three pops and one acceptance.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
aabbb stops at position four: no instruction at (q1, b, Z).

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Acceptance is two clauses: input exhausted, state final.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The same length costs 13 configurations when a middle has to be guessed.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The measured steps are 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 for n from one to six.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The peak stack height is n + 1, because the marker is always there.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
One pass, no copies: each symbol is read once and each stack symbol is touched twice.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
A general context-free language has no such guarantee.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Two criteria: end in a final state, or empty the stack.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
For a non-deterministic machine the two criteria define the same family.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
For a deterministic machine they differ, and the exact statement names the gap.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
a^n b^n passes the test; a*b* fails it, with a inside aa.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
The complement of a DCFL is a DCFL. That much is true.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The one-line proof is to swap the final states, and it is wrong.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Enumerated: 22 of the 31 strings of length at most four come out wrong.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The repair is a normal form: a machine that always consumes its input.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Seven operations preserve the class.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Six do not: union, intersection, concatenation, star, morphism, mirror image.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The CFL column is close to the opposite: union yes, complement no.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
One decidability consequence: "is L everything?" becomes decidable.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Witness one: the complement of a^n b^n c^n is context-free and not deterministic.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
If it were deterministic, its complement would be too, and that is not context-free.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Witness two: the even palindromes are unambiguous and still not deterministic.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
In the 64-string table, the two witness languages meet in the diagonal alone.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A language is deterministic exactly when it has an LR(1) grammar.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The shift-reduce run of aabb is a DPDA run: four shifts, two reductions.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
State, stack, shift, reduce: the correspondence is item by item.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
A shift-reduce conflict is two instructions in one cell.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Ask where the boundary is: a marker, a change of symbol, or nothing.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Ask what must be remembered: one count fits the stack, two comparisons do not.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Ask whether a guess is needed: if two hypotheses stay alive to the end, it is not a DCFL.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Four traps: the swap proof, the carried-over theorem, unambiguous, and regular.

